The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions and statements of its President on the situation in Somalia, in particular resolutions 2182 (2014), 2607 (2021), 2713 (2023), 2714 (2023), 2762 (2024), and 2775 (2025),

Reaffirming its full respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence, and unity of Somalia,

Determining that Al-Shabaab’s attempts to undermine peace, security, and stability in Somalia and the wider region, including through persistent acts of terrorism and the illicit acquisition of weapons and financial resources, constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Expressing grave concern over the ongoing flow of illicit arms and ammunition, as well as precursor chemicals and components used for improvised explosive devices (IEDs), into Somalia, and recognizing that interdicting these illicit flows at sea is essential to degrading Al-Shabaab’s operational capabilities,

Welcoming the efforts and leadership of the Federal Government of Somalia in countering terrorism, advancing its national security transition, and improving weapons and ammunition management, and underscoring the importance of enhanced cooperation between the Federal Government of Somalia, regional partners, and international naval forces to safeguard maritime security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew the provisions set out in paragraphs 15 and 17 of resolution 2182 (2014), and expanded by paragraph 5 of resolution 2607 (2021) to cover improvised explosive device (IED) components, as most recently renewed by resolution 2775 (2025), until 15 December 2025;

2. Reaffirms that the authorizations granted pursuant to paragraph 1 of this resolution apply only with respect to inspections carried out by Member States, acting nationally or through regional or international naval partnerships, in the territorial waters and on the high seas off the coast of Somalia, extending to and including the Arabian Sea and the Gulf of Aden, in accordance with international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea;

3. Calls upon all Member States conducting inspections under this authorization to coordinate closely with the Federal Government of Somalia, to respect its sovereignty, and to ensure that all relevant information regarding interdictions, seized vessels, weapons, ammunition, or IED components is promptly shared with the Federal Government of Somalia and reported without delay to the Committee established pursuant to resolution 751 (1992);

4. Encourages international partners to support the Federal Government of Somalia, at its request, in strengthening the capacity of the Somali Navy and Coast Guard to secure its maritime domain and combat illicit smuggling;

5. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

